.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt SOCKETCALL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm socketcall
.Nd dispatch a socket operation
.Sh SYNOPSIS
Linux i386 system call 102.
EAX is 102, EBX is the operation number, and ECX points to an array
of 32-bit arguments.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
implements the socket operations through one system call number.
The kernel copies the number of argument words required by the operation
from user memory in one checked read, then invokes the operation.
Musl declares no
.Fn socketcall
function; its socket functions issue this call when their direct system
call returns
.Er ENOSYS .
.Pp
The supported operations are listed below.
Arguments are in array order; pointers occupy one word.
.Bl -column "0x1000" "getsockname(2)" "Arguments"
.It Sy Number Ta Sy Operation Ta Sy Arguments
.It 1 Ta Xr socket 2 Ta domain, type, protocol
.It 2 Ta Xr bind 2 Ta fd, addr, addrlen
.It 3 Ta Xr connect 2 Ta fd, addr, addrlen
.It 4 Ta Xr listen 2 Ta fd, backlog
.It 5 Ta Xr accept 2 Ta fd, addr, addrlen
.It 6 Ta Xr getsockname 2 Ta fd, addr, addrlen
.It 7 Ta Xr getpeername 2 Ta fd, addr, addrlen
.It 8 Ta Xr socketpair 2 Ta domain, type, protocol, sv
.It 9 Ta Xr send 2 Ta fd, buf, len, flags
.It 10 Ta Xr recv 2 Ta fd, buf, len, flags
.It 11 Ta Xr sendto 2 Ta fd, buf, len, flags, addr, addrlen
.It 12 Ta Xr recvfrom 2 Ta fd, buf, len, flags, addr, addrlen
.It 13 Ta Xr shutdown 2 Ta fd, how
.It 14 Ta Xr setsockopt 2 Ta fd, level, option, value, len
.It 15 Ta Xr getsockopt 2 Ta fd, level, option, value, len
.It 16 Ta Xr sendmsg 2 Ta fd, msg, flags
.It 17 Ta Xr recvmsg 2 Ta fd, msg, flags
.It 18 Ta Xr accept4 2 Ta fd, addr, addrlen, flags
.It 19 Ta Xr recvmmsg 2 Ta fd, msgvec, vlen, flags, timeout
.It 20 Ta Xr sendmmsg 2 Ta fd, msgvec, vlen, flags
.It 0x1000 Ta Xr ping 2 Ta address
.El
.Pp
An address length for
.Fn accept ,
.Fn accept4 ,
.Fn getsockname ,
.Fn getpeername ,
or
.Fn recvfrom
is a pointer to a length.
The length for
.Fn getsockopt
is also a pointer.
Other lengths in the table are values.
.Pp
An operation not in the table returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Fn socketpair
makes only
.Dv AF_UNIX
pairs, and another domain returns
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
An operation on an
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket, or one that makes one, goes to the AF_UNIX implementation,
which
.Xr unix 7
describes.
.Pp
On an IPv4 or packet socket,
.Fn recvmsg
receives one datagram into a scatter list.
It reports the sender through
.Va msg_name
as a
.Vt sockaddr_in
for IPv4 or a
.Vt sockaddr_ll
for a packet socket, including its hardware address and packet type.
It supplies no ancillary data and sets
.Va msg_controllen
to zero; an
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket's
.Fn recvmsg
returns its control messages.
The DHCP client uses this call and checks the UDP checksum itself
when no
.Dv PACKET_AUXDATA
is returned.
.Pp
x86-64 has no
.Nm socketcall :
each operation is a call of its own, numbered in
.Xr syscalls 2 ,
with its arguments in the x86-64 registers in the order above;
.Nm accept
(43) is
.Nm accept4
without flags, and the two operations of
.Xr ping 2
are
.Nm rach2_ping
(400).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The kernel returns the selected operation's result, or a negative error
number.
Musl's public socket functions translate a negative error number to -1
and set
.Va errno .
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
uses operation 1 to create a UDP socket, operation 2 to bind it,
operation 11 to send a datagram, and operation 12 to receive it.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The required argument words cannot be read from user memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The operation number is not implemented.
.El
.Pp
Errors from the selected operation are returned unchanged.
Their conditions are documented on the corresponding pages.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr accept 2 ,
.Xr accept4 2 ,
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr getsockname 2 ,
.Xr listen 2 ,
.Xr ping 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr shutdown 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr unix 7
